---
title: Béchamel Sauce
description: Milk thickened with a white roux of butter and flour, seasoned with salt, pepper and nutmeg. The base for lasagne, Mornay sauce, croque monsieur, gratins and soufflés.
author: Daniel Gritzer
source: https://www.seriouseats.com/bechamel-white-sauce-recipe
course: sauce
cuisine: french
diet: vegetarian
difficulty: easy
servings: 4
yield: 250%ml
time: 10m
prep time: 0m
cook time: 10m
tags: [milk, roux, make-ahead]
---

Melt @butter{20%g}(unsalted) in a #small saucepan{} over medium-high heat. Don't let it brown.

Add @plain flour{12%g} and #whisk to a paste. Keep cooking and whisking ~{1%minute}, reaching into the corners so nothing scorches, until the raw flour smell is gone. The roux should stay pale: this is a white roux, not a toasted one.

Whisking constantly, add @whole milk{240%ml} in a thin, steady stream, or a couple of tablespoons at a time, whisking each addition in fully before adding more. The roux first seizes into thick clumps, then loosens; once all the milk is in, it will look as thin as plain milk. Cold milk is fine, as long as it goes in gradually.

Keep whisking over the heat ~{3%minutes} until it comes to a simmer and starts to thicken. Then lower the heat and cook, whisking, ~{3%minutes} more, until the sauce coats the back of a wooden spoon and you see the bottom of the pan as you whisk.

Season with @salt{to taste}, @black pepper{to taste} and @?nutmeg{to taste}(freshly grated). Whisk out any lumps; if they won't go, blitz briefly with a #stick blender{}.

> Use straight away, or press cling film onto the surface to stop a skin forming and keep warm. Keeps several days in a sealed container in the fridge; reheat very gently on the stove or in the microwave.

> Adjusting thickness: for a thinner sauce use 13 g butter and 8 g flour per 240 ml milk; for a thick one (soufflé base, moussaka) use 40 g butter and 22 g flour. Too thick at the end: whisk in more milk. Too thin: simmer it down, or whisk in a little extra roux cooked in a separate pan.

> White pepper instead of black keeps the sauce spotless.

-- Source "1 1/2 tbsp" butter and flour taken from its own gram weights (20 g, 12 g). The ~3 minutes to reach a simmer is estimated; the source gives no time.
